#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124170 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "37734955-23b7-52d1-881d-0c77f672c35a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3162" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y, z)= xyz+y^2.$ Find the best linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b, z-c \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,-1,1)+∇f(3,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(3, -1, 1)= -2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =yz\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{x}(3,-1,1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =xz+2y\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{y}(3,-1,1)=1 \\\\ {f}_{z} & =xy\\, &  & ⇒\\, &  & {f}_{z}(3,-1,1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(3, -1,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(3,-1,1) & =⟨-1,1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,-1,1)+∇f(3,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨-1,1,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2-(x-3)+(y+1)-3(z-1) \\\\  & =-2-x+3+y+1-3z+3 \\\\  & =5-x+y-3z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124338 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "839eb8fd-39b8-5f1f-b833-7134d39cdf2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8674" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,1) = 1, \\quad f_x(x,y) = 2xy, \\quad f_y(x,y) = x^2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.1, 0.9).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,1)+∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨{f}_{x}(1,1),{f}_{y}(1,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $f_x(1,1)$ and $f_y(1,1)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1)(1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =(1)^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx 1 + \\langle 2, 1 \\rangle \\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.1, 0.9)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.1$ and $y=0.9,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.1,0.9) & ≈1+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.1-1,0.9-1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.1,-0.1⟩ \\\\  & =1+0.2-0.1 \\\\  & =1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124340 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "54ac5be6-5b6a-58a3-a960-0d9f18e7fc88" :question/math-academy-id "e-8692" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y, z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,5)=2, \\quad  f_x(1,2,5)=4, \\quad  f_y(1,2,5)=1, \\quad f_z(1,2,5)=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,2.02,4.95).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b, c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b,z-c \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,5)+∇f(1,2,5)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,2,5)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,5),{f}_{y}(1,2,5),{f}_{z}(1,2,5)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.01,2.02,4.95)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.01,$ $y=2.02,$ and $z=4.95$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,2.02,4.95) & ≈2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,2.02-2,4.95-5⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨4,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.02,-0.05⟩ \\\\  & =2+0.04+0.02+0.1 \\\\  & =2.16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3068878-960b-5787-b74d-a95b430e4758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357211} {:answer/id #uuid "ef59c749-e193-5e7c-9c7b-1032889d7ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357212} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3f193e-e83c-5782-a3df-93ee78a86b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357213} {:answer/id #uuid "442f0b3d-ca3b-517b-be61-b040cc120396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357214} {:answer/id #uuid "a58fa64a-02a0-57fd-bb07-25f89f2e313c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3068878-960b-5787-b74d-a95b430e4758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357211} :answer-field/id #uuid "b33762f2-a50d-51c3-9206-90ba7216864a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16a5abbe-ff57-5b9a-ab1e-16d413797e0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129632" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)=-3,\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1)=-6,\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1)=3.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,-0.99).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.01,-0.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.01$ and $y=-0.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,-0.99) & ≈-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,-0.99+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-3-6(0.01)+3(0.01) \\\\  & =-3-0.06+0.03 \\\\  & =-3.03\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf475fe9-19b0-5dcf-ba6f-57d55775ec2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335313} {:answer/id #uuid "a205d2a5-0536-5cc9-838c-ee88f506562f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335314} {:answer/id #uuid "f701ad06-cc84-5692-a49a-6e14938f1ae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335315} {:answer/id #uuid "c149e568-46a9-5748-8ae0-00e42601552d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335316} {:answer/id #uuid "b063c0bc-5392-5647-a46f-51b432aedca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b063c0bc-5392-5647-a46f-51b432aedca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335317} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2687be3-a511-55fe-9d21-779b0fd19a63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5ffb4b6-2fa4-5a6f-a6dd-25f7f96b16a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-129634" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1)=1,\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1)=6.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(0.96,-0.95).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨1,6⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(0.96,-0.95)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.96$ and $y=-0.95,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.96,-0.95) & ≈-2+⟨1,6⟩\\cdot ⟨0.96-1,-0.95+1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨1,6⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.04,0.05⟩ \\\\  & =-2+1(-0.04)+6(0.05) \\\\  & =-2-0.04+0.3 \\\\  & =-1.74\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6ed3bd9-654a-526e-a5e8-911d195ef77a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357218} {:answer/id #uuid "f545b7a8-d561-581f-8f9e-4ee3512e215f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357219} {:answer/id #uuid "15a46d3c-9ce7-5186-878f-8a5555e987f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357220} {:answer/id #uuid "c27539c1-159a-5b5c-a31f-7db508ef8511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357221} {:answer/id #uuid "6f09d95f-6a2f-54a5-8720-087796bc0ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6ed3bd9-654a-526e-a5e8-911d195ef77a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357218} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a02d405-095b-5898-bb69-b4ed45b0373a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e7dfc5c-c144-5489-a8d0-c27c73e72883" :question/math-academy-id "q-129636" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,3)=4,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+y,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=x.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,3),$ estimate the value of $f(1.04,3.03).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,3)+∇f(1,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩ \\\\  & ≈f(1,3)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,3),{f}_{y}(1,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩ \\\\  & ≈4+⟨{f}_{x}(1,3),{f}_{y}(1,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,3)$ and ${f}_{y}(1,3)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,3) & =2(1)+3=5 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,3) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.04,3.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.04$ and $y=3.03,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.04,3.03) & ≈4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.04-1,3.03-3⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.04,0.03⟩ \\\\  & =4+5(0.04)+1(0.03) \\\\  & =4+0.2+0.03 \\\\  & =4.23\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49aecb4a-dad0-5a2b-85d6-19b9a6d5ba6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357225} {:answer/id #uuid "e62457ba-0612-5aef-a2eb-f78e4a462739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357226} {:answer/id #uuid "becbcc96-86c2-5d37-bec2-864aa6793601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357227} {:answer/id #uuid "52813a06-58d8-5f29-bd42-9bfba352992f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.94" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357228} {:answer/id #uuid "19ace5d3-cace-5e78-a701-76bd57fc3430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "becbcc96-86c2-5d37-bec2-864aa6793601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357227} :answer-field/id #uuid "85ba5f1c-48bc-517a-9060-647184b38aa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "722c5cd7-1610-5083-b573-d3d05eef7bb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-129637" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=2xy-{y}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}-2xy.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(0.95,-1.04).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & ≈f(1,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,-1)$ and ${f}_{y}(1,-1)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-1) & =2(1)(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-3 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,-1) & ={1}^{2}-2(1)(-1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈-2+⟨-3,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(0.95,-1.04)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.95$ and $y=-1.04,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.95,-1.04) & ≈-2+⟨-3,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.95-1,-1.04+1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨-3,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.05,-0.04⟩ \\\\  & =-2-3(-0.05)+3(-0.04) \\\\  & =-2+0.15-0.12 \\\\  & =-1.97\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc4350fc-cd39-55f9-88f7-6b03cd394f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357232} {:answer/id #uuid "074b5d17-1966-5602-a208-7d61a55fe866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357233} {:answer/id #uuid "760f544e-0628-525a-8251-c31e865fbb76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357234} {:answer/id #uuid "39e99e97-023d-5ea3-92f3-933e829deb51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357235} {:answer/id #uuid "01995fbd-842b-5fd2-be5b-65087f7de464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "760f544e-0628-525a-8251-c31e865fbb76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357234} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ccc4ee4-3761-518d-acc4-76247a58f8b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc7ad887-70cb-5594-8df2-c5717f843d45" :question/math-academy-id "q-129655" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(-1,1,0)=-1,\\,{f}_{x}(-1,1,0)=1,\\,{f}_{y}(-1,1,0)=-2,\\,{f}_{z}(-1,1,0)=0.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,1,0)$ estimate the value of $f(-1,1.01,-0.01)$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(-1,1,0)+∇f(-1,1,0)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),y-1,z-0⟩ \\\\  & =f(-1,1,0)+⟨{f}_{x}(-1,1,0),{f}_{y}(-1,1,0),{f}_{z}(-1,1,0)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-1,z⟩ \\\\  & =-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-1,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1,1.01,-0.01)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-1,$ $y=1.01,$ and $z=-0.01$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,1.01,-0.01) & ≈-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1+1,1.01-1,-0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0.01,-0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-1+1(0)-2(0.01)+0(-0.01) \\\\  & =-1+0-0.02+0 \\\\  & =-1.02\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0670a411-5c45-573d-ac2c-a29cf26f6c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335320} {:answer/id #uuid "30975257-6cd2-587b-ae1d-24e9a2b543cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335321} {:answer/id #uuid "663ab1c6-310d-5204-8090-ab68c1cd5285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335322} {:answer/id #uuid "8973736e-b698-5d4d-a77d-5e998b86e220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335323} {:answer/id #uuid "eb18256a-7cdc-544e-8ee3-5316b2a50486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0670a411-5c45-573d-ac2c-a29cf26f6c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335320} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc124bfe-a409-5d29-b2e6-f65d217c76e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "726b98f5-3113-547b-995d-4663e2498149" :question/math-academy-id "q-129657" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=6,\\,{f}_{x}(1,2,3)=6,\\,{f}_{y}(1,2,3)=3,\\,{f}_{z}(1,2,3)=2.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ estimate the value of $f(1.08,1.93,3.05)$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,3)+∇f(1,2,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,3),{f}_{y}(1,2,3),{f}_{z}(1,2,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.08,1.93,3.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.08,$ $y=1.93,$ and $z=3.05$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.08,1.93,3.05) & ≈6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.08-1,1.93-2,3.05-3⟩ \\\\  & =6+⟨6,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.08,-0.07,0.05⟩ \\\\  & =6+6(0.08)+3(-0.07)+2(0.05) \\\\  & =6+0.48-0.21+0.1 \\\\  & =6.37\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a1322ae-e430-5629-91b0-4fa9cb4d578c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357239} {:answer/id #uuid "9568e8d8-78e0-5e08-9b74-73c17a96dedc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357240} {:answer/id #uuid "20129a8f-9dd3-5c10-9cc8-a1d3415fa1dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357241} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa0adb4-1a7d-5b8b-bade-a90b105503fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357242} {:answer/id #uuid "39eda29a-ea27-5aa1-b0f5-e6c79817307f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fa0adb4-1a7d-5b8b-bade-a90b105503fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357242} :answer-field/id #uuid "495c7a33-6298-5ac7-b2b1-ce8c34153360" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "851c10b8-d992-54aa-ac89-df5795dc4190" :question/math-academy-id "q-129661" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(0,1,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=z{e}^{x},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(0.01,1.01,-0.99).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,1,-1)+∇f(0,1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-0,y-1,z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨{f}_{x}(0,1,-1),{f}_{y}(0,1,-1),{f}_{z}(0,1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(0,1,-1)$, ${f}_{y}(0,1,-1)$ and ${f}_{z}(0,1,-1),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1,-1) & =(-1){e}^{0}=-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,1,-1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{z}(0,1,-1) & ={e}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z+1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(0.01,1.01,-0.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.01,y=1.01$ and $y=-0.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.01,1.01,-0.99) & ≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,1.01-1,-0.99+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2-1(0.01)-1(0.01)+1(0.01) \\\\  & ≈-2-0.01-0.01+0.01 \\\\  & ≈-2.01\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "943bbf47-0de5-5945-9649-ae7bf940998d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-2+x-y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357246} {:answer/id #uuid "21943c76-a14b-5c48-843e-92d06b0a7737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈2+x+y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357247} {:answer/id #uuid "c7fc4900-f94d-512a-81e9-bae99e9605ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈1+x+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357248} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1e83fe-f2e5-5a1b-82de-f56b614ba214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈2+x+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357249} {:answer/id #uuid "72d635ea-d802-53ae-81c1-0f633e0575e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-2y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "943bbf47-0de5-5945-9649-ae7bf940998d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-2+x-y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357246} :answer-field/id #uuid "622e5437-cc3a-5c20-992b-b46594be4c14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "15c1d2a5-42db-5b28-be3e-79c32c67c3c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-129748" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,-1,-1)=1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =yz\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1)=1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =xz\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =xy\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,-1,-1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+(x-1)-(y+1)-(z+1) \\\\  & ≈-2+x-y-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca80878a-70ff-5206-93ee-c8c94fb25cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) \\approx \\ln 2 + \\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{y - 1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122567} {:answer/id #uuid "ede89334-fcb0-5875-b253-d149639aa9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) \\approx \\ln 2 - 1 + x + y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122568} {:answer/id #uuid "358b4117-667a-5671-b3c6-6592dc3890b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) \\approx \\ln 2 + \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{y - 1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122569} {:answer/id #uuid "72a298f4-9d67-59f4-b5dc-f6859920540a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) \\approx \\ln 2 + \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{y - 1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122570} {:answer/id #uuid "7f969e7b-2b40-5360-8b00-f9f778bfd41d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) \\approx \\ln 2 - \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{y - 1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72a298f4-9d67-59f4-b5dc-f6859920540a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) \\approx \\ln 2 + \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{y - 1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122570} :answer-field/id #uuid "585a3835-018c-5119-b629-13dcb0d6478a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ed320f7-bb9c-5ef9-a5ac-93165087275f" :question/math-academy-id "q-36747" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x+y).$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,1)+∇f(0,1)\\cdot ⟨x,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(0,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =\\frac{1}{1+x+y}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(0,1)=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =\\frac{1}{1+x+y} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(0,1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(0,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(0,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,1)+∇f(0,1)\\cdot ⟨x,y-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{y-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a72e800-6065-5b81-b422-d9da3e9826d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357253} {:answer/id #uuid "039035cb-0a1d-5d09-a999-bbc766ac3109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357254} {:answer/id #uuid "01b5d356-4ec1-56b9-9410-d4d22c3b9d96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357255} {:answer/id #uuid "497cd82c-2d94-5099-a900-434b458fbb38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357256} {:answer/id #uuid "cfed4045-f8f3-57b5-9683-add678491a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfed4045-f8f3-57b5-9683-add678491a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357257} :answer-field/id #uuid "deeeb20d-1e8d-548f-bb45-93126ea658d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea4d33e7-7c09-5d5e-9ccf-5bec677f88d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-98326" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(2,1)=2,\\,{f}_{x}(2,1)=-1,\\,{f}_{y}(2,1)=-1.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.98,0.94).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(2,1)+∇f(2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =f(2,1)+⟨{f}_{x}(2,1),{f}_{y}(2,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.98,0.94)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.98$ and $y=0.94,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.98,0.94) & ≈2+⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.98-2,0.94-1⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.02,-0.06⟩ \\\\  & =2+(-1)(-0.02)+(-1)(-0.06) \\\\  & =2+0.02+0.06 \\\\  & =2.08\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7966efe5-c29e-59c1-bfea-79dd3032d2b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357260} {:answer/id #uuid "81193eed-d5bf-5a37-9525-9749e07d51d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357261} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0f1729-cb8d-5fcf-9310-362f020dd48f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357262} {:answer/id #uuid "115ae366-e4b2-5b9d-803b-15cc78653af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357263} {:answer/id #uuid "9a70f8c4-0580-5598-98d2-a7c1d3529d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7966efe5-c29e-59c1-bfea-79dd3032d2b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357260} :answer-field/id #uuid "830a98bf-d229-53db-9248-dcecf3b8e9e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e291dc27-d17d-5da1-907a-8c20b4b06f0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98344" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & f(-3,-1,-2)=1, &  & {f}_{x}(-3,-1,-2)=4, \\\\  & {f}_{y}(-3,-1,-2)=-3, &  & {f}_{z}(-3,-1,-2)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-3,-1,-2)$ estimate the value of $f(-3.04,-1.02,-2.03).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-3,-1,-2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(-3,-1,-2)+∇f(-3,-1,-2)\\cdot ⟨x+3,y+1,z+2⟩ \\\\  & =f(-3,-1,-2) \\\\  & \\,+⟨{f}_{x}(-3,-1,-2),{f}_{y}(-3,-1,-2),{f}_{z}(-3,-1,-2)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+3,y+1,z+2⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨4,-3,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+3,y+1,z+2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-3.04,-1.02,-2.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-3.04,$ $y=-1.02,$ and $z=-2.03$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3.04,-1.02,-2.03) & ≈1+⟨4,-3,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3.04+3,-1.02+1,-2.03+2⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨4,-3,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.04,-0.02,-0.03⟩ \\\\  & =1-0.16+0.06+0.06 \\\\  & =0.96\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67885177-68dc-55cb-8839-4576cc313a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335369} {:answer/id #uuid "8b7841f5-cf7f-5712-9062-5317389e0236" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335370} {:answer/id #uuid "03007d32-556b-5252-b21a-ed92dbe88436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335371} {:answer/id #uuid "b9453ff8-2af8-5651-af0f-7977ae7f73d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335372} {:answer/id #uuid "20bd1252-138f-5b02-bf93-781c065b83d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20bd1252-138f-5b02-bf93-781c065b83d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335373} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7311abe-11fe-5247-ad21-d4ddf394da3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46ef7f70-d725-51e8-9995-25f1cbb2c776" :question/math-academy-id "q-98348" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2,-3)=-6, & \\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=yz,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=xz,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)=xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,-3),$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,1.95,-3.02).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,-3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,-3)+∇f(1,2,-3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z+3⟩ \\\\  & =-6+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,-3),{f}_{y}(1,2,-3),{f}_{z}(1,2,-3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z+3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,2,-3)$, ${f}_{y}(1,2,-3)$ and ${f}_{z}(1,2,-3),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,2,-3) & =2(-3)=-6 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,2,-3) & =1(-3)=-3 \\\\ {f}_{z}(1,2,-3) & =1(2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-6+⟨-6,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z+3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.01,1.95,-3.02)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.01,y=1.95$ and $z=-3.02,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,1.95,-3.02) & ≈-6+⟨-6,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,1.95-2,-3.02+3⟩ \\\\  & =-6+⟨-6,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,-0.05,-0.02⟩ \\\\  & =-6-0.06+0.15-0.04 \\\\  & =-5.95\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba8fb6d-3a05-5c68-94a7-afc4fd13f558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈\\frac{x}{2}+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357267} {:answer/id #uuid "c34967f9-9b3f-5690-b5ac-b2c3c64e7a9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈16+2x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357268} {:answer/id #uuid "5df35a1e-5afa-5cc0-8391-941ebc0f1dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2x+\\frac{y}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357269} {:answer/id #uuid "7c95ec5f-6cc6-5b77-bf20-a9d5f5c26f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈\\frac{x}{2}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357270} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2510ee-62d1-50fb-acc2-214392fa1bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈16+2x+\\frac{y}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5df35a1e-5afa-5cc0-8391-941ebc0f1dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)≈2x+\\frac{y}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357269} :answer-field/id #uuid "70f953ec-0ee9-5cad-9b70-336e5ef135b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f4fd5df-dadd-52f6-b149-36a5758ba585" :question/math-academy-id "q-98551" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{xy}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,8).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,8)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(2,8)+∇f(2,8)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(2,8)=8.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{xy}}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(2,8)=\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 8}}=2, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{xy}} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(2,8)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 8}}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(2,8)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,8) & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(2,8)+∇\\,f(2,8)\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-8⟩ \\\\  & =8+⟨2,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-8⟩ \\\\  & =8+2(x-2)+\\frac{1}{2}(y-8) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d74d46b-6884-58ee-bd81-cf6d47da9785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1+x+y-\\frac{z}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357274} {:answer/id #uuid "76c74212-a7f1-5863-afa4-dfc22129b560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1-x+y-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357275} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0408c7-99e8-5f0b-8756-0bc0fc4e9e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈1-x+y-\\frac{z}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357276} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4724d5-c5d2-5523-88f1-5a62d4ce31ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-3+x-y+\\frac{z}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357277} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4dd43e-e6bc-5d74-a641-91df1c9064a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-3+x-y-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d74d46b-6884-58ee-bd81-cf6d47da9785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈-1+x+y-\\frac{z}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357274} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece05490-41ce-5a5b-95af-f814f72055e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f0a65ec3-c24d-50c1-a1d2-f9666a246122" :question/math-academy-id "q-98554" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1,e)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,1,e)+∇f(1,1,e)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,1,e)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =y\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(1,1,e)=1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(1,1,e)=1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =-\\frac{1}{z}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{z}(1,1,e)=-\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,1,e)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,1,e) & =⟨1,1,-\\frac{1}{e}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,1,e)+∇f(1,1,e)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩ \\\\  & =0+⟨1,1,-\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩ \\\\  & =0+1(x-1)+1(y-1)-\\frac{1}{e}(z-e) \\\\  & =-1+x+y-\\frac{z}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3edb51c-8bc1-5e43-a79b-4891054f59cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈6-x-2y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335390} {:answer/id #uuid "1b35f050-d0ac-57fe-b923-292fbcac9a24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈8+x-2y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335391} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ae3250-fa99-5810-ba7d-fc870a104f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈6-x+y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335392} {:answer/id #uuid "fae03db1-edc5-5270-8134-a60989997d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈8-x+y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335393} {:answer/id #uuid "33064134-d89c-5a2d-9cf7-56b27083cd39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈6+x+2y-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3edb51c-8bc1-5e43-a79b-4891054f59cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)≈6-x-2y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335390} :answer-field/id #uuid "a212bf02-5f2e-5905-860c-5dcbb5c5bde6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec12843c-221e-5977-accb-d9e9aa80cb79" :question/math-academy-id "q-98555" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{9}{x+2y-z}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,1,1)+∇f(2,1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(2,1,1)=3.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =-\\frac{9}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(2,1,1)=-\\frac{9}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-\\frac{18}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(2,1,1)=-\\frac{18}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=-2, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =\\frac{9}{(x+2y-z)^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(2,1,1)=\\frac{9}{(2+2(1)-1)^{2}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(2,1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1,1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(2,1,1),{f}_{y}(2,1,1),{f}_{z}(2,1,1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,1,1)+∇f(2,1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =3+⟨-1,-2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y-1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =3-1(x-2)-2(y-1)+1(z-1) \\\\  & =6-x-2y+z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
